GeoJSON and KML

Characteristics
GeoJSON (.geojson) and KML (Keyhole Markup Language, .kml) share the following characteristics:

They work with the geometry types you already know, such as points, lines and polygons.
A file contains a collection of features. It may contain different geometry types (for example points and polygons). QGIS shows them as separate layers when you import the file and asks which ones you want to add.
They use longitude and latitude in WGS 84 (EPSG:4326).
They are text files, so working with large files in QGIS can be slow.

The main difference is the syntax: GeoJSON uses JSON, while KML uses XML. A compressed KML file is called a KMZ file (.kmz).
This is what a file with a point (Ghent) and a polygon (the Vrijdagmarkt, a square in Ghent) looks like in GeoJSON:
{
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "features": [
    {
      "type": "Feature",
      "geometry": {
        "type": "Point",
        "coordinates": [3.7174, 51.0543]
      },
      "properties": { "name": "Ghent" }
    },
    {
      "type": "Feature",
      "geometry": {
        "type": "Polygon",
        "coordinates": [[[3.7250, 51.0569], [3.7259, 51.0565], [3.7266, 51.0570], [3.7257, 51.0574], [3.7250, 51.0569]]]
      },
      "properties": { "name": "Vrijdagmarkt" }
    }
  ]
}






Try it yourself: From map to text





In QGIS
You open these files in exactly the same way as a shapefile or a GeoPackage: drag the file into QGIS, or use the Vector tab of the Data Source Manager (see Importing data into QGIS).






As with a CSV file, it is a good idea to save the layer as a GeoPackage before you start working with it (Export ▸ Save Features As…, see Working with GeoPackages). Editing is then faster and safer, and you can store a style with the layer.



Sometimes you need GeoJSON or KML yourself. QGIS can also export to them: right-click the layer, choose Export ▸ Save Features As… and under Format choose GeoJSON or Keyhole Markup Language [KML]. Set the CRS to EPSG:4326 - WGS 84, the coordinate system these formats expect.
